From 1ad324b104459d40b63c3746484d22c151b85030 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <theunbounddeveloper@outlook.com>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 14:12:12 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] first boot: fix the factory Wi-Fi key actually landing, and
 register the banner

Three defects in the previous commit, two of which reaper_verify caught and one
of which it could not.

1. THE FACTORY WI-FI WAS STILL OPEN - the whole point of the change did not
   land. router_defaults[] carries the generic wl_* template AND a later,
   explicit per-unit block (wl0_*..wl3_*). Those are different keys, so setting
   the template overwrote nothing: every radio still got wpa_psk="",
   auth_mode_x=open, crypto=tkip+aes. The per-unit entries are now set too, each
   under the same WL_UNIT_6G guard the template block uses so the siblings stay
   correct - 6 GHz is WPA3-only and is not the same radio index on every model.
   Found by tracing where the wireless page actually writes the PSK, prompted by
   the question "what if a user sets their password back to admin?".

2. ej_reaper_wifi_default() compared every radio against nvram_default_get of
   the GENERIC wl_wpa_psk. With (1) unfixed that meant comparing a radio holding
   "" against "admin" - not equal - so the banner reported Wi-Fi as ALREADY
   SECURED on a factory box, the exact inverse of the truth. It now reads each
   radio's own per-unit default, which is the value that radio was seeded from,
   so it stays correct regardless of which table entry wins.

3. Reaper_SecBanner.asp was not in reaper_langcheck.py OUR[], so nothing checked
   its tokens - the LANGCOVERAGE assertion caught it on the first verify, which
   is what it is for. Registered, and the stale Reaper_FirstBoot.asp entry
   removed there and in _port_protect.sh / port_sibling_v2.sh.

Note on (1) and (2): they masked each other. A test that only asked "does the
banner clear when I set a Wi-Fi password" would have passed on a box that had
never had a factory key at all.
